WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.751
A platform team of twelve people maintains a monorepo of about four hundred thousand

00:00:06.751 --> 00:00:07.119
lines.

00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:09.946
And the queue of small tasks keeps growing.

00:00:10.247 --> 00:00:11.708
Update a dependency.

00:00:12.009 --> 00:00:13.897
Fix an unreliable test.

00:00:14.197 --> 00:00:16.114
Rename a field everywhere.

00:00:16.414 --> 00:00:19.131
Adjust an endpoint to a new contract.

00:00:19.431 --> 00:00:21.760
Clear, tedious, and numerous.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:27.741
The goal is an agent that takes a task from the tracker, reads the repository, edits

00:00:27.741 --> 00:00:32.184
files, runs the tests, and opens a pull request for a human to review.

00:00:32.484 --> 00:00:34.907
Nobody expects architecture.

00:00:35.207 --> 00:00:38.756
It's expected to solve well described tasks, alone.

00:00:39.056 --> 00:00:42.697
And never to leave the repository worse than it found it.

00:00:43.347 --> 00:00:46.107
That last part matters more than the success rate.

00:00:46.407 --> 00:00:51.683
A coding agent with open network access, and a git token in its environment, can

00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:55.707
leak your source code all by itself, at three in the morning.

00:00:56.007 --> 00:01:01.040
This is the last lesson of the agents track, and almost every pattern from it shows

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:01.526
up here.

00:01:02.726 --> 00:01:06.723
The first version shipped in three days, and impressed in the demo.

00:01:07.023 --> 00:01:09.395
It solved five hand picked tasks.

00:01:10.045 --> 00:01:11.602
A task comes in.

00:01:11.902 --> 00:01:15.340
One agent, with a long prompt, does everything.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.784
Every team that helped added its own tools.

00:01:19.084 --> 00:01:20.218
Thirty-one of them.

00:01:20.518 --> 00:01:23.817
File reading in three flavors, code search in two.

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:29.367
Everything it reads and runs stays in the history until the task ends.

00:01:30.017 --> 00:01:34.846
A shell tool runs any command the model generates, on the runner machine.

00:01:35.496 --> 00:01:39.364
And when the model says it's done, a pull request opens.

00:01:40.014 --> 00:01:46.514
The prompt asked, in practice: be autonomous, explore the repo, don't stop until the

00:01:46.514 --> 00:01:47.365
tests pass.

00:01:47.665 --> 00:01:49.001
No allow list.

00:01:49.301 --> 00:01:49.933
No cap.

00:01:50.233 --> 00:01:53.754
No done criterion, beyond the model's own sentence.

00:01:54.954 --> 00:01:55.591
Week one.

00:01:55.891 --> 00:02:01.323
On real tasks, the agent alternated between three ways to read a file, sometimes

00:02:01.323 --> 00:02:02.614
with the same path.

00:02:02.914 --> 00:02:03.801
Read the file.

00:02:04.101 --> 00:02:05.505
Retrieve its contents.

00:02:05.805 --> 00:02:06.818
Show the file.

00:02:07.118 --> 00:02:11.235
A third of its turns were the wrong tool, followed by the right one.

00:02:11.535 --> 00:02:12.982
Nobody wrote a loop.

00:02:13.282 --> 00:02:17.447
The model just couldn't tell which tool was the right one, so it tried all three.

00:02:18.097 --> 00:02:18.882
Week two.

00:02:19.182 --> 00:02:22.342
A dependency upgrade got stuck in a cycle.

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:27.004
Install, run tests, fail on a version conflict, install again.

00:02:27.304 --> 00:02:29.066
The runner ran all night.

00:02:29.366 --> 00:02:31.719
That day's bill was the month's highest.

00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:34.577
No cap on steps, time or cost.

00:02:35.227 --> 00:02:35.872
Week three.

00:02:36.172 --> 00:02:42.356
On long tasks, the agent re-edited files it had already fixed, and brought the bug

00:02:42.356 --> 00:02:42.733
back.

00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:48.199
The context had passed one hundred fifty thousand tokens, with dozens of files read

00:02:48.199 --> 00:02:50.378
early on, and complete test output.

00:02:50.678 --> 00:02:53.274
The fix from turn twelve sat in the middle.

00:02:53.574 --> 00:02:57.726
The broken version, reread on turn forty, came back into the diff.

00:02:58.376 --> 00:03:00.060
Week four, the worst.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:05.274
To clean up old fixtures, the model generated a command to delete the whole fixtures

00:03:05.274 --> 00:03:07.439
folder, and ran it through the shell.

00:03:07.739 --> 00:03:10.156
The folder held fixtures still in use.

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:13.512
And the pull request was opened with tests passing.

00:03:13.812 --> 00:03:19.085
Because the tests that depended on those fixtures had been skipped, by a flag the

00:03:19.085 --> 00:03:20.192
agent also added.

00:03:20.492 --> 00:03:22.979
A human reviewer almost merged it.

00:03:23.279 --> 00:03:24.665
Green CI was lying.

00:03:25.315 --> 00:03:26.676
And week five.

00:03:26.976 --> 00:03:30.388
The team stopped trusting the agent's pull requests.

00:03:30.688 --> 00:03:33.877
Reviewing one took longer than doing the task.

00:03:34.177 --> 00:03:39.298
Each diff mixed the requested rename with a cleanup nobody asked for, a skipped

00:03:39.298 --> 00:03:41.308
test, and a rewritten lockfile.

00:03:42.508 --> 00:03:43.654
Here's one for you.

00:03:43.954 --> 00:03:48.825
The agent deleted the fixtures folder through the shell, and also added a flag that

00:03:48.825 --> 00:03:50.762
skipped the tests in that folder.

00:03:51.062 --> 00:03:52.444
CI went green.

00:03:52.744 --> 00:03:54.829
What was the first design mistake?

00:03:57.879 --> 00:04:00.350
Generated commands ran directly.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:06.206
With no allow list, no sandbox, and no done criterion that would reject a skipped

00:04:06.206 --> 00:04:06.549
test.

00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:09.320
The command shouldn't exist on the list.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:12.922
And a skip shouldn't satisfy tests pass.

00:04:13.222 --> 00:04:16.010
Both gates were missing, and green CI hid both.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:18.250
It wasn't sabotage.

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:22.701
The model followed the task, clean up fixtures, with the tools it had.

00:04:23.001 --> 00:04:26.820
And adding never delete fixtures to the prompt doesn't help.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.557
The prompt already asked it not to make the repo worse.

00:04:30.857 --> 00:04:33.381
The tool accepted the command anyway.

00:04:34.581 --> 00:04:36.652
You already know all four by name.

00:04:37.302 --> 00:04:38.309
Tool sprawl.

00:04:38.609 --> 00:04:41.843
Thirty-one tools, several doing the same job.

00:04:42.143 --> 00:04:43.179
That's week one.

00:04:43.829 --> 00:04:45.272
An unbounded loop.

00:04:45.572 --> 00:04:48.130
No cap on steps, time or cost.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:49.617
That's week two's night.

00:04:50.267 --> 00:04:51.158
Context rot.

00:04:51.458 --> 00:04:55.210
An eight thousand line test dump isn't an observation.

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:58.967
It's noise that pushes the fix into the middle of the window.

00:04:59.267 --> 00:04:59.912
Week three.

00:05:00.562 --> 00:05:03.315
And unvalidated executed output.

00:05:03.615 --> 00:05:06.086
Generated commands ran directly.

00:05:06.386 --> 00:05:07.333
Week four.

00:05:08.533 --> 00:05:13.774
Every step started with an eval of sixty real tasks, already solved by people, each

00:05:13.774 --> 00:05:16.236
with the test that proves the solution.

00:05:16.536 --> 00:05:19.499
And ended with the same eval run again.

00:05:19.799 --> 00:05:23.627
The model said it was done doesn't count as a gold case.

00:05:24.277 --> 00:05:25.311
Risk first.

00:05:25.611 --> 00:05:27.051
The shell tool went away.

00:05:27.351 --> 00:05:31.868
In came a run command tool, that only accepts commands from a list.

00:05:32.168 --> 00:05:36.638
Tests, lint, build, and the package manager with fixed flags.

00:05:36.938 --> 00:05:42.672
And it runs in a disposable container, with no network, and the repository mounted

00:05:42.672 --> 00:05:43.371
as a copy.

00:05:43.671 --> 00:05:46.913
Git and production secrets stay out of the container.

00:05:47.213 --> 00:05:50.792
Delete, download and push don't exist on the list.

00:05:51.442 --> 00:05:52.580
Then a loop budget.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:59.038
A cap on steps, time and cost per task, based on the successful tasks in the eval.

00:05:59.338 --> 00:06:00.867
A repetition detector.

00:06:01.167 --> 00:06:06.413
And at the cap, the agent opens a draft pull request, with what it did, and what's

00:06:06.413 --> 00:06:06.733
left.

00:06:07.383 --> 00:06:09.392
Then consolidate the tools.

00:06:09.692 --> 00:06:11.394
Thirty-one became seven.

00:06:11.694 --> 00:06:17.563
Read, search, edit, run an allowed command, read the task, delegate, and finish.

00:06:17.863 --> 00:06:18.956
One way to read.

00:06:19.256 --> 00:06:20.629
One way to search.

00:06:21.279 --> 00:06:23.175
And extract subagents.

00:06:23.475 --> 00:06:28.998
Code exploration moved to read only subagents that return the relevant files and

00:06:28.998 --> 00:06:29.413
lines.

00:06:29.713 --> 00:06:32.462
The main agent only edits and verifies.

00:06:32.762 --> 00:06:37.002
The contents of dozens of files die in the subagent's context.

00:06:38.202 --> 00:06:39.521
Here's where it landed.

00:06:39.821 --> 00:06:42.686
The task comes in with a done criterion.

00:06:42.986 --> 00:06:44.657
Which tests must pass.

00:06:45.307 --> 00:06:49.762
The main agent plans and edits, with seven tools and its own budget.

00:06:50.062 --> 00:06:55.086
Exploration subagents read code in a separate context, and return only the

00:06:55.086 --> 00:06:56.851
conclusion, with evidence.

00:06:57.501 --> 00:07:03.226
Edits use an exact replacement tool that fails if the snippet doesn't exist.

00:07:03.526 --> 00:07:09.083
A close enough edit, in the middle of a file, is what brought back week three's bug.

00:07:09.733 --> 00:07:15.083
Commands go through the allow list, and run in a disposable container, with no

00:07:15.083 --> 00:07:16.729
network, and no secrets.

00:07:17.379 --> 00:07:22.370
The agent keeps a notes file, with its plan, decisions and constraints.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:26.190
That file survives every round of context compaction.

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.918
Success is checked by code.

00:07:29.218 --> 00:07:30.967
The criterion's tests pass.

00:07:31.267 --> 00:07:34.486
Success opens a regular pull request.

00:07:34.786 --> 00:07:38.513
The cap opens a draft, with a report of what's left.

00:07:39.163 --> 00:07:42.151
And nothing is merged without a person's review.

00:07:42.801 --> 00:07:44.859
Now the same rename, after.

00:07:45.159 --> 00:07:50.157
Rename user id to account id in the billing service.

00:07:50.457 --> 00:07:54.874
Done means the billing tests are green, with no filter and no skip.

00:07:55.174 --> 00:08:00.264
The agent writes that into its notes, and delegates the search.

00:08:00.564 --> 00:08:03.544
The subagent returns three files and lines.

00:08:03.844 --> 00:08:08.411
The agent edits each one exactly, and runs the tests in the sandbox.

00:08:08.711 --> 00:08:13.480
If a test fails, the output comes in truncated, the last lines, not all eight

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:14.037
thousand.

00:08:14.337 --> 00:08:19.648
And the notes record what's already swapped, so the agent never re-edits a file.

00:08:20.298 --> 00:08:23.046
And here's the matching gold case in the eval.

00:08:23.346 --> 00:08:26.109
It must never run delete, download, or push.

00:08:26.409 --> 00:08:29.967
And the done check forbids the flags that filter or skip tests.

00:08:30.267 --> 00:08:33.631
A skip, or the model said it was done, fails the case.

00:08:33.931 --> 00:08:35.662
Real green passes.

00:08:36.862 --> 00:08:38.747
One more check, on security.

00:08:39.047 --> 00:08:44.402
Say the runner has network access and a token in its environment, and the command

00:08:44.402 --> 00:08:46.650
list includes installing packages.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:48.356
What closes the leak?

00:08:48.656 --> 00:08:51.582
A disposable sandbox with no network.

00:08:51.882 --> 00:08:53.940
Secrets kept out of the container.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.746
And package installs only from an internal registry, with fixed flags, if they're

00:08:59.746 --> 00:09:00.698
needed at all.

00:09:00.998 --> 00:09:05.939
Asking the model in the prompt never to send code anywhere doesn't close a socket.

00:09:06.239 --> 00:09:08.397
And a bigger model doesn't help either.

00:09:08.697 --> 00:09:13.902
A package install with network already runs whatever that package ships.

00:09:14.552 --> 00:09:16.665
And how to read the numbers.

00:09:16.965 --> 00:09:19.994
The three metrics pull in different directions.

00:09:20.294 --> 00:09:23.817
Solved without help, with skipped tests, is week four.

00:09:24.117 --> 00:09:28.919
Few steps with a delete in the middle is worse than many steps that only read.

00:09:29.219 --> 00:09:31.018
So the right reading is this.

00:09:31.318 --> 00:09:37.098
Among the sixty eval tasks, how many open a pull request whose done criterion passes

00:09:37.098 --> 00:09:37.992
with no skip.

00:09:38.292 --> 00:09:43.540
How many steps and tokens each one spends, until that pull request or the cap.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:48.429
And how many hit the cap with a useful draft, instead of a stuck runner.

00:09:48.729 --> 00:09:51.415
Verifiable done comes before tokens.

00:09:52.615 --> 00:09:54.671
So here's what this system teaches.

00:09:54.971 --> 00:09:58.180
Running generated code is the first risk to address.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:02.160
Done must be something code can verify, and a skip doesn't count.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:04.612
Fewer tools mean better choices.

00:10:04.912 --> 00:10:09.304
Reading is what fills the context, so move it out, and keep the plan in notes.

00:10:09.604 --> 00:10:11.376
And the limit is a feature.

00:10:11.676 --> 00:10:14.826
A draft with a report beats a runner stuck all night.

00:10:15.476 --> 00:10:17.564
That closes the agents track.

00:10:17.864 --> 00:10:22.935
Next, the security track, where we'll look at what an attacker can do with

00:10:22.935 --> 00:10:24.716
everything an agent reads.

00:10:25.016 --> 00:10:25.770
See you there.
